Fix D6: the tab that would not close was Zen's empty-tab placeholder

Patch 0034's feature has been blocked since 2026-08-01 by a tab that
gBrowser.removeTab() would not remove. It stayed in gBrowser.tabs with
`closing: true` and a null linkedBrowser, so anything that later walked the
tab list dereferenced that null browser -- surfacing as `browserElement is
null` and a window the user would find broken.

The zombie is Zen's `zen-empty-tab`: the placeholder shown when a workspace
holds nothing. It is furniture, not content, and was never part of the
restored session. Zen treats it that way itself -- `#isLastTabInWindow` skips
`zen-empty-tab` when deciding whether a window still has anything in it
(tabbrowser-js.patch). Handing it to removeTab() does not remove it; it
half-destroys it. The cleanup was trying to close Zen's own furniture.

The fix is to exclude structural tabs from the sweep, from the "is the window
now empty" count, and from survivor selection -- focusing the placeholder
would leave the user on a blank workspace with their pinned tabs unselected
beside it. `zen-glance-tab` (a transient preview overlay) is excluded by the
same reasoning; only the empty tab was actually measured.

Two corrections to the earlier diagnosis, both worth carrying forward:

  * D6 was recorded as a STARTUP failure. It is not. The zombie reproduces
    mid-session with no restart involved, which is how it was finally caught
    -- the restart cycle had made every iteration cost minutes and hidden how
    ordinary the trigger was.
  * The earlier work went looking for what `_endRemoveTab` was waiting on.
    Nothing was waiting. removeTab was being handed a tab it was never meant
    to receive, and the animation gate was a red herring.

Measured, mid-session, five tabs: every real tab closed cleanly while the
`zen-empty-tab` came back `stillInTabs: true`. After the fix, the same
scenario gives zombieCount 0, the pinned tab survives and takes focus, and
the placeholder is left alone.

Full quit/relaunch acceptance test, the one 0034's header asks for:

  before quit    about:robots/PIN, about:license/PIN, and four unpinned tabs
  after relaunch about:robots/PIN, about:license/PIN, and the placeholder
  zombies 0, selectedBrowser valid, sessionstore.jsonlz4 written (3021 bytes)

That last number matters: D0's signature failure was `sessionstore.jsonlz4`
being ABSENT, so its presence is what separates this from the data-loss bug.

The pref `kavacha.session.clear-unpinned-on-quit` still defaults to FALSE and
should stay that way until a human has lived with it. This is the only
Kavacha behaviour that discards user data on an ordinary action, and the
feature working is not the same as the feature being wanted on by default.
